WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.328
Hi, I'm Pixel!

00:00:02.328 --> 00:00:06.482
Voxel Musou: one hero against a voxel army, in a browser tab.

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:11.528
A fixed pool of three hundred slots.

00:00:11.528 --> 00:00:15.334
A director keeps about eighty-four on the hero, and recycles the dead.

00:00:18.592 --> 00:00:21.385
Every soldier is a slot in a pool sized once at boot.

00:00:21.805 --> 00:00:24.568
Every twenty frames, a director counts fighters.

00:00:24.568 --> 00:00:27.725
Under eighty-four, the nearest waiting block marches in.

00:00:28.145 --> 00:00:32.282
A fallen soldier's slot frees three and a half seconds later, for reinforcements.

00:00:32.702 --> 00:00:35.416
The enemies setting only changes how many wait.

00:00:35.416 --> 00:00:37.111
The fight stays eighty-four strong.

00:00:38.592 --> 00:00:41.929
Here is that fight, in the project's own screenshot: the hero at

00:00:41.929 --> 00:00:43.598
the centre of a dense crowd.

00:00:44.018 --> 00:00:47.228
Top right, the minimap: a red cluster around a white arrow.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.704
Each frame banks its time, capped at a tenth of a second.

00:00:52.124 --> 00:00:55.825
It spends that in sixtieth-of-a-second steps, at most four per frame.

00:00:56.245 --> 00:00:56.817
Still behind?

00:00:56.817 --> 00:01:00.247
It drops the rest, so a slow machine plays in slow motion.

00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:01.722
Then it draws.

00:01:01.722 --> 00:01:04.182
Two seeded random streams make fights repeatable.

00:01:05.733 --> 00:01:09.145
Each hit freezes the hero a few frames, which would drag the

00:01:09.145 --> 00:01:10.282
combo off its beat.

00:01:10.282 --> 00:01:11.419
One rule fixes that.

00:01:11.839 --> 00:01:13.846
Move T: frames the move has played.

00:01:14.886 --> 00:01:17.029
Stop: frames spent frozen by hitstop.

00:01:18.127 --> 00:01:21.411
Absorb is eight: up to eight frozen frames count as played.

00:01:21.831 --> 00:01:24.915
So the next strike starts on time, even in a ring of twenty.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:31.339
From the move table: strikes twenty-five, twenty-six, twenty-four and twenty-six frames apart,

00:01:31.339 --> 00:01:32.935
then a late thirty-five.

00:01:34.308 --> 00:01:37.398
The feel is data: a pool, a clock and a frame table

00:01:37.398 --> 00:01:38.686
you can check by hand.

00:01:42.108 --> 00:01:45.932
A recycled pool, a clock that slows instead of spiralling, and hitstop

00:01:45.932 --> 00:01:47.207
folded into the beat.

00:01:47.627 --> 00:01:49.772
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:49.772 --> 00:01:50.384
I'm Pixel.

00:01:50.384 --> 00:01:50.691
Bye!
